💡 Intuition
Each city can be viewed as a node in an undirected graph, where isConnected[i][j] == 1 indicates a direct connection between cities i and j.

A province is simply a connected component in this graph. Starting a BFS from any unvisited city will visit every city that belongs to the same province. Therefore, every time we encounter an unvisited city, we perform a BFS to mark all cities in that connected component as visited and increment our province count.

Since the graph is represented as an adjacency matrix, we determine the neighbors of a city by scanning its entire row.

🚀 Approach
Maintain a visited set to keep track of explored cities.
Iterate through every city.
If a city has not been visited:
Start a BFS from that city.
During BFS, scan the corresponding row of the adjacency matrix to find all directly connected neighbors.
Add every unvisited connected city to the queue and mark it as visited.
Once the BFS completes, all cities in that province have been explored, so increment the province count.
Return the total number of provinces.

⏱️ Complexity Analysis
Time Complexity: O(n²)
For each visited city, we scan all n columns of its row in the adjacency matrix.
Across all BFS traversals, each city is processed once, resulting in n × n = O(n²).
Space Complexity: O(n)
The visited set stores up to n cities.
The BFS queue can contain up to n cities in the worst case.

Code
from collections import deque
class Solution:
    def findCircleNum(self, isConnected: List[List[int]]) -> int:
        visited = set()
        provinces = 0
        n = len(isConnected)

        def bfs(city):
            queue = deque([city])
            visited.add(city)
            while queue:
                current = queue.popleft()
                for neighbor in range(n):
                    if isConnected[current][neighbor] == 1 and neighbor not in visited:
                        visited.add(neighbor)
                        queue.append(neighbor)

        for city in range(n):
            if city not in visited:
                bfs(city)
                provinces += 1
        return provinces
